<file path="docs/governance/autonomous-mode.md">
# Autonomous Mode

**Opt-in only.** This contract applies to long-running, semi-autonomous agent work where the human is not actively reviewing each edit — e.g., scheduled batch refactors, multi-PR cleanup runs, or any flow where an agent will produce a PR end-to-end without intermediate prompts. Routine interactive work uses `AGENTS.md` only.

The full default principles in `AGENTS.md` still apply. This file adds operational discipline for cases when there's no live human in the loop.

## When this applies

- An agent is explicitly told to operate "autonomously" or "until done."
- A PR is being opened by an automated process or scheduled job.
- The work will produce multiple commits before any human review.
- The maintainer is offline / will only see the result via the merged PR.

When in doubt, the **default is NOT autonomous mode**. Operate via `AGENTS.md` and ask for confirmation on high-risk decisions.

## 1. Issue-first workflow

Before changing any versioned file in autonomous mode:

1. Locate or create a `task issue` in GitHub.
2. Label it with at least `kind:task`, one `status:*`, one `priority:*`, one `area:*`, one `risk:*`, one `lane:*`.
3. Declare in the issue body:
   - `Owner atual`
   - `Lane oficial`
   - `Workspace da task`
   - `Write-set esperado`
   - `Classificacao de risco`
4. Create a worktree at `.claude/worktrees/<lane>/<issue-number>-<slug>/`.
5. Work in branch `task/<issue-number>-<slug>` (repo root stays on `main`).
6. Open a PR with `Closes #<issue-number>` in the body.
7. Complete via `scripts/pr_complete.ps1 -Pr <number>` or equivalent: wait for checks, merge, verify issue closure.
8. Update issue + docs before marking task complete.

## 2. Lanes

Official lanes:

- `lane:frontend` — `apps/web/**`
- `lane:backend` — `apps/api/**`, `src/**`, `desktop/**`, `dashboard/**`, `tests/**`
- `lane:ops-quality` — `.github/**`, `docs/**`, root docs, operational scripts
- `lane:master` — cross-cutting execution lane with broad write access

**Rule:** `1 task = 1 owner = 1 branch = 1 worktree = 1 PR`.

A task belongs to exactly one lane. If the work genuinely spans two product lanes, split into separate child tasks.

## 3. Risk classifications

| Class | Definition | PR Setup |
|---|---|---|
| `risk:safe` | Isolated write-set | Open as ready |
| `risk:shared` | Touches shared files or critical-runtime paths | Open as **draft** |
| `risk:contract-sensitive` | Public API, DB schema, or public docs | Open as **draft** + document compatibility |

Critical-path classes are enforced by `.github/guardrails/path-policy.json`. Paths in `shared-governance`, `critical-bootstrap`, `critical-runtime`, or `critical-contract` require the matching `lane:*` and `risk:*`.

## 4. Parallel work protocol

- The protocol is per-task, not per-agent identity.
- If two tasks share write-set, one must wait, reduce scope, or be marked dependent.
- Record dependencies in the issue before continuing.
- During parallel work, public interfaces follow `additive-only` by default.
- Breaking contract changes require a dedicated `risk:contract-sensitive` task with serialized merge.

## 5. Child tasks between lanes

When your lane needs changes in another lane's write-set, open a formal child task instead of editing across lanes.

Child task body must include:

- `Task mae` (parent task number)
- `Lane solicitante` (your lane)
- `Criterio de consumo` (how the parent will verify the delivery)
- `Write-set esperado` (files the child lane will change)

While the child task is open or in review, the parent task is `status:blocked`. After the child merges, the parent moves to `status:awaiting-consumption`. Only the requester lane marks the delivery consumed and unblocks the parent.

## 6. Commit, push, merge policy

- Commit at coherent, validated checkpoints.
- Push when a checkpoint should be preserved remotely or reflected in the PR.
- Open the PR (even as draft) as soon as the branch is reviewable.
- When validations pass: update the issue, mark the PR ready if needed, and merge via `scripts/pr_complete.ps1` or equivalent.
- Preferred merge: **squash**.
- After merge: confirm task closure, remove the local worktree, confirm the remote branch was removed.

## 7. CI enforcement

In autonomous mode, the heavy guardrails are active:

- `.github/workflows/pr-issue-guardrails.yml` enforces the issue/PR contract when the PR carries the `flow:autonomous` label.
- `.github/workflows/perf-guardrails.yml` runs on touched runtime paths.
- `.github/guardrails/path-policy.json` enforces lane/risk constraints on critical paths.

For interactive PRs (no `flow:autonomous` label), these checks are skipped or relaxed.

## 8. Stop conditions

Autonomous mode stops immediately and requests human input when:

- A high-risk decision is reached (architecture, data model, auth, destructive ops).
- A critical-path file is touched without matching lane/risk authorization.
- A contract-sensitive breaking change is required.
- Tests fail and the failure isn't reproducible/explainable.
- The agent cannot validate the change against the project's success criteria.
</file>

<file path="docs/perf/performance_guardrails.md">
# Performance Guardrails

Issue: `#138`
Owner lane: `ops-quality`

This repository now keeps a lightweight, repeatable baseline for:

- main product web routes:
  `/`, `/empresas`, `/empresas/[cd_cvm]`, `/setores`, `/setores/[slug]`
- hot API endpoints behind those routes:
  `/companies`, `/companies/filters`, `/companies/suggestions`, `/companies/{cd_cvm}`,
  `/companies/{cd_cvm}/years`, `/companies/{cd_cvm}/statements`,
  `/companies/{cd_cvm}/kpis`, `/sectors`, `/sectors/{slug}`

## What is measured

### Web

- rendering mode from `next build` (`static` vs `dynamic`)
- `revalidate_seconds` from `.next/prerender-manifest.json`
- `first_load_uncompressed_js_bytes` per route from `.next/diagnostics/route-bundle-stats.json`

### API

- in-process HTTP latency against a synthetic 449-company SQLite dataset
- median and p95 for the hot endpoints listed above

The API harness is intentionally synthetic and local. It is good for regression detection inside CI, not for production SLO claims.

## Source of truth

- Baseline + budgets: [performance_baseline.json](./performance_baseline.json)
- Collector/checker: [`scripts/perf_guardrail.py`](../../scripts/perf_guardrail.py)
- Existing deeper query benchmark: [benchmark_read_paths.md](./benchmark_read_paths.md)

## How to run locally

From the repo root:

```powershell
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
python -m pip install -r apps/api/requirements-dev.txt
npm ci --prefix apps/web
python scripts/perf_guardrail.py check --baseline docs/perf/performance_baseline.json
```

To refresh the baseline after an intentional performance change:

```powershell
python scripts/perf_guardrail.py capture --output docs/perf/performance_baseline.json
```

## Guardrail policy

- Rendering mode changes are blocked unless the baseline is updated intentionally.
- Revalidate changes for static routes are blocked unless the baseline is updated intentionally.
- Web bundle growth is allowed only up to the per-route ceiling stored in the baseline JSON.
- API latency is allowed only up to the per-endpoint `max_median_ms` and `max_p95_ms` ceilings stored in the baseline JSON.

These budgets are intentionally light. They are meant to catch meaningful regressions, not micro-variations.

## When to refresh the baseline

Refresh `docs/perf/performance_baseline.json` only when:

- a route intentionally changes rendering mode
- a static route intentionally changes `revalidate`
- a deliberate product change legitimately increases route JS
- an approved backend change legitimately shifts the hot endpoint latency envelope

If the baseline is refreshed, include the reason in the PR body and update issue evidence.
</file>

<file path="docs/reference/gics-import.md">
# GICS Import

This project must not infer GICS classifications from CVM, B3, ticker, company
name, or custom sector labels. CVM/B3/native labels are useful metadata, but
they are not GICS. Treating them as GICS creates false sectors and misleading
counts.

GICS is populated only from an explicit mapping file approved by the project
owner.

## Expected File

Place the approved file at:

```text
data/import/gics_mapping.csv
```

Required CSV columns:

```csv
cd_cvm,gics_sector,gics_sub_industry,source,as_of_date
```

Optional diagnostic columns:

```csv
ticker,company_name,cnpj,notes
```

Rules:

- `cd_cvm` is the only key used for database updates.
- `gics_sector` must be one of the 11 official GICS sector labels used by the app.
- `gics_sub_industry` must be present and non-empty.
- `source` must identify the approved source.
- `as_of_date` must use `YYYY-MM-DD`.
- `ticker`, `company_name`, and `cnpj` are diagnostics only. They can help
  report possible matches or mismatches, but they do not authorize updates.

## Commands

Dry-run is the default. It validates the CSV, matches by `cd_cvm`, writes a
report, and does not update the database:

```powershell
python scripts/import_company_gics.py
```

Apply mode is explicit:

```powershell
python scripts/import_company_gics.py --apply
```

Sanity-check mode does not require the CSV. It checks the current schema,
company table size, current GICS counts, and writes the same report file:

```powershell
python scripts/import_company_gics.py --check
```

The report is written to:

```text
data/reports/gics_backfill_report.json
```

## Report Fields

The report includes:

- mappings read
- invalid rows
- duplicate `cd_cvm` rows
- matched companies
- unmatched mappings
- fallback-key diagnostics, without applying those matches
- companies that would be updated in dry-run
- companies updated in apply mode
- companies skipped and skip reasons
- companies still unclassified
- distinct GICS sectors after import
- distinct GICS sub-sectors after import
- source values found
- as-of dates found
- current physical `gics_*` field counts in the DB

In dry-run/apply import modes, `companies_still_unclassified` and the
"after import" sector lists are based on the approved CSV coverage, not on old
physical `gics_*` values already present in the database. This matters during
recovery because old non-null GICS fields may be untrusted. The report also
shows the current physical DB field counts separately for diagnostics.

In `--check` mode, no approved CSV is loaded, so the summary describes the
current physical DB fields only.

## Universe Check

GICS import updates only companies already present in `companies`. It does not
add companies and does not touch `financial_reports`.

Before applying a full mapping, confirm that `companies` represents the full CVM
active company universe. The importer reports when the active-universe cache is
larger than `companies`.

If the universe is incomplete, seed or reconcile `companies` first, then rerun:

```powershell
python scripts/seed_catalog.py --dry-run
python scripts/seed_catalog.py
python scripts/import_company_gics.py --check
```

## Safety Warning

Never use `setor_cvm`, `setor_analitico`, B3 sector labels, ticker names, or
normalized company names as GICS. They can be displayed as their own metadata
only when clearly labeled, but they must not be mixed into GICS sector or
sub-sector views.
</file>

<file path="docs/reference/interface-map.md">
# Interface Map - Frontend routes x API endpoints

**Leitura obrigatoria antes de tocar `apps/web` ou `apps/api`.**

Este documento e a fonte de verdade sobre quais rotas o frontend tem, qual e o
status de cada uma, e quais endpoints de API cada rota consome. Qualquer IA ou
pessoa que trabalhe em qualquer uma das duas areas deve atualizar este arquivo
**antes** de comecar a implementacao.

> Contratos HTTP detalhados: `docs/V2_API_CONTRACT.md`
> Hierarquia de rotas e IA de produto: `docs/SITEMAP.MD`
> Requisitos de fase e scope: `docs/V2_PHASE2_WEB_SLICE.md`

---

## Como manter este documento

- **Nova rota no frontend**: adicione a secao, marque como `em desenvolvimento`,
  liste os endpoints necessarios. Se algum nao existir ainda, registre em
  [Pendencias de backend](#pendencias-de-backend).
- **Novo endpoint no backend**: adicione na [tabela inversa](#tabela-inversa-endpoint-x-rotas-que-o-usam).
  Verifique se alguma rota em _Pendencias_ esperava por ele e atualize o status.
- **Rota vai ao ar**: mude o status de `em desenvolvimento` para `live`.
- **Endpoint alterado ou removido**: verifique a tabela inversa, atualize todas as
  rotas afetadas, registre breaking change no PR.

---

## Rotas do frontend

### PG-01 - `/` (Home) `live`

**Objetivo**: roteador de entrada - leva o usuario ao caminho de analise mais curto.

**Endpoints consumidos**:

| Endpoint | Params | Para que serve |
|---|---|---|
| `GET /health` | - | Trust strip / sinal de saude da API |
| `GET /companies` | `page=1&page_size=8` | Sugestoes rapidas de empresas na home |
| `GET /companies/suggestions` | `q=`, `limit=` | Autocomplete com fallback para catalogo CVM |

**Notas de implementacao**:
- o hero consulta `GET /companies/suggestions` diretamente
- quando a busca local nao encontra resultados suficientes, o autocomplete pode
  complementar itens do catalogo CVM para abrir o detalhe on-demand

---

### PG-02 - `/empresas` (Companies Hub) `live`

**Objetivo**: descoberta e selecao de empresa por busca e filtro de setor.

**Endpoints consumidos**:

| Endpoint | Params | Para que serve |
|---|---|---|
| `GET /companies` | `search=`, `sector=`, `page=`, `page_size=` | Lista paginada de empresas |
| `GET /companies/filters` | - | Opcoes de setor para o filtro |

**Query params publicos da rota**: `?busca=&setor=&pagina=`

---

### PG-03 - `/empresas/[cd_cvm]` (Company Detail) `live`

**Objetivo**: analise completa de uma empresa - KPIs, demonstracoes e contexto.

**Endpoints consumidos**:

| Endpoint | Params | Para que serve |
|---|---|---|
| `GET /companies/{cd_cvm}` | - | Metadados da empresa (header) |
| `GET /companies/{cd_cvm}/years` | - | Anos anuais disponiveis (seletor de periodo) |
| `GET /companies/{cd_cvm}/statements` | `stmt=DRE\|BPA\|BPP\|DFC`, `years=2023,2024` | Aba Demonstracoes |
| `GET /companies/{cd_cvm}/kpis` | `years=2023,2024` | Aba Visao Geral |
| `GET /companies/{cd_cvm}/export/excel` | - | Download do workbook Excel completo da empresa, sempre com todos os anos disponiveis |
| `POST /companies/{cd_cvm}/request-refresh` | - | Dispara bootstrap/refresh on-demand da empresa |
| `GET /refresh-status` | `cd_cvm=` | Polling do status de refresh e da barra estimada de progresso/ETA |

**Query params publicos da rota**: `?anos=2023,2024&aba=visao-geral\|demonstracoes&stmt=DRE\|BPA\|BPP\|DFC`

Notas de contrato:
- o seletor de anos e anual-only; `1Q/2Q/3Q` sem anual nao entram em `anos`
- colunas trimestrais continuam dentro das tabelas e KPIs quando o ano anual
  selecionado possui periodos derivados/disponiveis
- `4Q` depende do anual `YYYY` combinado com a base trimestral do mesmo ano
- no bundle de KPIs trimestrais, `4Q` e deduplicado contra `YYYY`; o periodo
  explicito permanece visivel na aba Demonstracoes
